-- title:
--   Zariski gluing of sections over a finite basic-open cover
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a commutative ring and let $f : A \to \operatorname{Spec} S$ be a morphism from a scheme $A$ to the spectrum of $S$. Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $c : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to S$ be such that the ideal generated by the $c_i$ is all of $S$. For each $i$ let $L_i$ be a commutative $S$-algebra which is a localisation of $S$ away from $c_i$, and for each pair $(i,j)$ let $L_{ij}$ be a commutative $S$-algebra which is a localisation of $S$ away from $c_i c_j$; let $l_{ij} : L_i \to L_{ij}$ and $r_{ij} : L_j \to L_{ij}$ be ring homomorphisms compatible with the structure maps, i.e. $l_{ij} \circ (S \to L_i) = (S \to L_{ij})$ and $r_{ij} \circ (S \to L_j) = (S \to L_{ij})$. Then two assertions hold. First (separatedness of the functor of points): if $s, s' : \operatorname{Spec} S \to A$ satisfy $f \circ s = \mathrm{id}$ and $f \circ s' = \mathrm{id}$, and $s$ and $s'$ have equal restrictions along $\operatorname{Spec} L_i \to \operatorname{Spec} S$ for every $i$, then $s = s'$. Second (gluing): given morphisms $s_i : \operatorname{Spec} L_i \to A$ with $f \circ s_i$ equal to the canonical map $\operatorname{Spec} L_i \to \operatorname{Spec} S$ for all $i$, and with $s_i$ and $s_j$ agreeing after precomposition with $\operatorname{Spec} l_{ij}$ and $\operatorname{Spec} r_{ij}$ respectively for all $i,j$, there exists a section $\mathrm{sec} : \operatorname{Spec} S \to A$ with $f \circ \mathrm{sec} = \mathrm{id}$ whose restriction along $\operatorname{Spec} L_i \to \operatorname{Spec} S$ is $s_i$ for every $i$. Uniqueness of the glued section is not part of the second conjunct, but follows from the first.
--
--   This is the Zariski sheaf property of the functor of points $T \mapsto \operatorname{Hom}(T, A)$, specialised to the finite cover of an affine base $\operatorname{Spec} S$ by the basic opens $D(c_i)$ and phrased entirely in ring-theoretic terms, with arbitrary chosen models of the localisations away from $c_i$ and $c_i c_j$. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfel'd part of the development to produce points and level structures over an affine base from compatible local data, for instance in [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_strata_point_locally_corr_of_span_eq_top`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_strata_point_locally_corr_of_span_eq_top).
